# The recording half. One counter, advanced wherever the program reads the
# keyboard, across four sites:
#   $FD1B - the ROM's KEYIN wait loop (keys typed at the BASIC prompt)
#   $741F - the game's control-select delay loop poll
#   $7890 - the game's wait-for-space loop
#   $6217 - the game's own real-time keyboard ingest: LDA $C000, and on a key
#           it clears the strobe (STA $C010) and fills the ring buffer at
#           $623C. This is the one that actually takes the key from the
#           hardware, not merely peeks at it, and it is what feeds the
#           in-game I/J/K/M movement dispatch. Confirmed empirically, not
#           just by static trace: recording play.keys (23 keys) with only
#           the first three sites yields 11 records (the BASIC-prompt phase
#           only); adding $6217 yields all 23. Without it the coordinate has
#           a hole and the entire game phase goes undelivered, not merely
#           cycle-quantised -- see docs/plans/2026-08-14-probe-stamped-keys-plan.md
#           task 4 and its report for the experiment.
#
# play.probe must install at exactly these sites and count exactly this way.
# Nothing enforces that; a mismatch shows up as a failed replay.
counter n

probe kb() {
  inc n
  record n
}

install kb at $FD1B, $741F, $7890, $6217
